  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>Between the vertebrae lie discs — shock absorbers. Outside a disc has a strong shell, the <strong>annulus fibrosus</strong>; inside, a dense, springy <strong>nucleus</strong>. It is like a stiff pad with a filling.</p>
    <p>When we often bend forward, lift heavy things, sit for long periods or bend the head over a phone, pressure on the front of the disc rises and the nucleus gradually shifts backwards. At some point the annulus tears, the nucleus comes out and starts pressing on a nerve root or the spinal cord. This is a herniation.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Protrusion</strong> — the disc bulges but the annulus is still intact. A common age-related MRI finding that usually does not hurt by itself.</li>
      <li><strong>Herniation (extrusion)</strong> — part of the nucleus has come out beyond the annulus.</li>
      <li><strong>Sequestration</strong> — a fragment of the nucleus has broken off and lies separately. It sounds frightening, but sequestered fragments often shrink on their own.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <p>Nerves from the lumbar spine go to the leg, from the cervical spine to the arm. So with a herniation it is not so much the back that hurts as the limb:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Lumbar spine</strong> — lower back pain radiating to the buttock, thigh, calf or foot.</li>
      <li><strong>Cervical spine</strong> — neck pain radiating to the shoulder, shoulder blade, arm; numb fingers.</li>
      <li><strong>Thoracic spine</strong> — rare: back pain, a feeling of tightness in the chest.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Typically the pain worsens with coughing, sneezing and straining. Numbness, "pins and needles" and <strong>weakness</strong> may appear: hard to stand on toes or heels, things drop from the hand.</p>
    <h3>Not every back pain is a herniation</h3>
    <p>More often the back hurts because of muscle spasm and strain, overload of the facet joints or the sacroiliac joint, inflammation where ligaments attach. Such pain does not radiate below the knee and is not accompanied by weakness — and it is treated differently.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Examination by a neurologist or neurosurgeon</strong> — the most important. I check strength, sensation and reflexes and understand which root is affected and whether the pain comes from the spine.</li>
      <li><strong>Spine MRI</strong> — the main method. It shows the herniation and whether it presses on a nerve, and helps rule out other causes: tumours, inflammation.</li>
      <li><strong>CT</strong> — if MRI cannot be done or bone needs assessing.</li>
      <li><strong>X-ray</strong> does not show a herniation, but flexion-extension views help assess vertebral instability.</li>
      <li><strong>Nerve conduction studies</strong> — when it is unclear whether it is a herniation or, for example, an entrapment syndrome.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>An MRI report is not a verdict or a diagnosis.</strong> Sometimes the report is full of frightening terms, yet the person feels well and needs no surgery. And sometimes there is a single line — and severe pain that cannot be managed without surgery. The decision is based on complaints, examination and the images themselves, not just the report text.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Non-surgical treatment</h2>
    <p>If there is no weakness in the arm or leg, no bladder or bowel problems, and the pain eases with treatment, we start with conservative treatment:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Medication</strong> — anti-inflammatories, painkillers, muscle relaxants and other drugs. The specific regimen is prescribed by a doctor.</li>
      <li><strong>Physiotherapy and massage</strong> — reduce spasm and improve circulation.</li>
      <li><strong>Exercise therapy</strong> — strengthens muscles and unloads the spine. I showed a set of back pain exercises <a href={`${tg}/42`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">on my Telegram channel</a>, and which sports are acceptable with a herniation <a href={`${tg}/33`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">in a separate post</a> (in Russian).</li>
      <li><strong>A nerve block</strong> — targeted injection of medication to the inflamed root under X-ray or ultrasound guidance. It relieves swelling and pain and makes rehabilitation possible. More about <a href={`${tg}/49`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">radicular blocks</a> (in Russian).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A herniation can shrink on its own. In my practice there is a patient whose extruded disc fragment noticeably shrank on a follow-up MRI three months after a flare-up, stopped pressing on the roots, and the pain went away without surgery — <a href={`${tg}/229`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">before and after images on the channel</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="operaciya">
    <h2>When surgery is needed</h2>
    <p>I consider surgery if MRI shows the herniation compressing a root or the spinal cord and at least one of these applies:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>pain has not gone for more than 3 months despite treatment;</li>
      <li>weakness in a leg or arm has appeared or is increasing;</li>
      <li>bladder or bowel function is disturbed, numbness in the groin area has appeared — this is <strong>cauda equina syndrome</strong>, which needs emergency surgery.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Size alone does not decide: not every large herniation needs surgery, and not every small one can be treated conservatively.</p>
    <h3>How surgery is done</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Microdiscectomy</strong> — removal of the herniation through a small incision under the microscope. The nerve is freed and the rest of the disc preserved.</li>
      <li><strong>Endoscopic removal</strong> — through punctures with a camera.</li>
      <li><strong>Spinal fusion</strong> — the vertebrae are joined with a metal construct. Needed in selected cases — with disc destruction or vertebral instability.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Most often the operation is done under epidural anaesthesia: you are awake but feel no pain. Sometimes under general anaesthesia. It takes 30–60 minutes on average. Discharge is usually a few days after surgery.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="vosstanovlenie">
    <h2>Recovery</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>For 2–4 weeks</strong> do not sit for long or lift heavy things while bending. Sitting briefly — to eat, to use the toilet — is fine.</li>
      <li><strong>A lumbosacral brace</strong> — about 4–6 hours a day: when sitting and under load. It is not needed lying down.</li>
      <li><strong>The wound</strong>: the dressing is usually removed on day 4 and the wound treated with an antiseptic for a few days.</li>
      <li><strong>Physical activity</strong>: start with exercise therapy, ideally supervised by a physiotherapist. Move to active training gradually, usually after 2–3 months.</li>
      <li><strong>Residual symptoms.</strong> Mild discomfort in the first month is a normal part of recovery. Numbness and weakness, if the nerve was compressed for long, may take months to fade and sometimes partly remain.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="srochno" slot="urgent">
    <h2>When to seek urgent help</h2>
    <div class="callout alert">
      <p>If numbness appears in the groin area, bladder or bowel function is disturbed, or weakness in a leg or arm increases rapidly — <strong>call emergency services immediately (112; 103 in Russia).</strong> This may be nerve compression that needs surgery within hours.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
